  "worked_solution": "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258850/assets/eb99763da194e2e21ccb8032f049be9deb2f94605b70878968ccda1bc093c04c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\\infty .\n$$",
